"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[776],{7776:(e,t,a)=>{a.d(t,{NX:()=>n,_n:()=>o});let o=[{slug:"letters-from-the-moon",title:"Letters from the Moon",italic:"from the",kicker:"A Correspondence",deck:"A curious correspondence from beyond our skies.",by:"Orion Belle",role:"Keeper of Undeliverable Letters, the Night Post House",minutes:8,img:"/images/scenes/gaz-moon.webp",alt:"An owl carrying a sealed letter across a full moon",caption:"The owl that delivered the seventh letter would not leave until it had been read aloud.",teaser:"A series of enigmatic letters, said to have come from the Moon itself, has arrived at the Night Post House on Wick Street, written on paper unlike any found in the mortal world. Their messages are brief but profound, and every one is signed with a single silver star.",epigraph:"Everyone looks at the moon. Almost no one thinks to write.",body:["The first letter came on a Tuesday, which is not a day on which anything remarkable is supposed to happen. It was lying on the sorting table in the Night Post House when I unlocked the door, although the door had been locked all night and the windows painted shut since before the war. The envelope was cold. Not chilly, the way paper is in November, but cold the way a stone is cold at the bottom of a well, and when I turned it over it left a faint silver dust on my fingers that would not wash off for three days.","It was addressed, in a hand so fine it looked engraved, to The One Who Paints Me, c/o the Night Post House, Wick Street.","I assumed, as you would, that it was a joke. The owls who fly our night rounds are a mischievous lot, and so are the boys who feed them. But nobody owned up, and when I held the paper to the lamp I saw that it was not paper at all. It was thinner than onion skin and it shone faintly, the way frost shines before anyone has walked on it.",{letter:{date:"The first night of the waning",text:"I do not know your name. I only know that every night for a hundred and fifteen years a light has burned in your attic window, and someone at that window has painted me. Not the way the astronomers draw me, all grey numbers and craters. You paint me the way I feel. Last night you stopped. Are you ill? I have so few friends.",sign:"✦"}},"The lamp attic of the Post House has not been used since 1911. I went up with a candle. Under a sheet, under a century of dust, there was an easel, a chair and a stack of small canvases tied with a faded ribbon. Every one of them was the moon. Hundreds of moons: a crescent like a fingernail, a full moon swollen and orange over the rooftops, a moon half-hidden behind a chimney as if it were shy. The last canvas was only half painted. In the corner of each one, in tiny gold letters, was the name M. Vell.",{head:"The girl in the attic"},"Marguerite Vell was the last lamp-girl the Post House ever had. The ledgers say she was hired at fourteen to keep the attic lamp burning, so that the night owls could find their way home with the post, and that she never once went home before midnight. They say she was quiet and plain and talked to no one. And they say that on the night of the winter solstice, 1911, she climbed the stairs to the attic and was never seen again. Her coat was still on its hook in the morning. The window was open. There was frost on the sill in the shape of a footprint, a small one, pointing out.","I wrote back. I did not know what else to do. I wrote that Marguerite was gone, that she had been gone for more than a hundred years, and that I was very sorry. I left the letter on the attic sill under a saucer of milk, which my grandmother always said was the polite thing to leave for anything that comes in the night. In the morning the milk was gone and the saucer was full of frost.",{letter:{date:"The second night of the waning",text:"Gone? But she promised. She was nine years old when she first wrote to me. She burned her letter in a candle so the smoke would carry it up, and it did. She asked me whether I was lonely. No one had ever asked me that. I told her the truth: that everyone looks at me, and no one ever looks back. She said that she would. Every night. For as long as she lived. Tell me, little postman: how long do people live?",sign:"✦"}},{quote:"Everyone looks at me, and no one ever looks back."},"I could not bring myself to answer that one. For four nights I sat in the attic with the half-painted canvas, and I am ashamed to say I wept over it, because I understood something then that I had not understood before. The moon had been counting on Marguerite. Not on her paintings, on her. On the small square of yellow light in a window, on the one face out of all the millions in the world that turned upward on purpose.",{image:{src:"/images/scenes/gaz-owl-ink.webp",alt:"An engraved owl in flight",side:"right"}},"On the fifth night the owl came. A barn owl, white as a plate, with a letter held in its beak, and it landed on the easel and fixed me with its black eyes and would not move. I understood that I was meant to read it aloud. So I did, with my voice shaking, to an owl, in an attic, at three in the morning.",{letter:{date:"The night before the new moon",text:"I have been looking for her. It is not hard for me to look; I see everything that faces the sky. And I have found her, little postman. She did not die. On the night of the solstice she climbed out of the window and walked up the frost to me, because I asked her to, because I was selfish and I could not bear to lose her to a short human life. She has been here all this time. She paints me from the inside now. Look tonight, at the dark part of me, at the edge. You will see her lamp.",sign:"✦"}},{head:"The lamp at the edge"},"There was no moon that night. It was the new moon, the dark of the month, when the sky is at its blackest. I stood on the roof of the Post House with a pair of brass opera glasses borrowed from the clockmaker next door, feeling like a fool. And then, low over the river, where the moon should have been and was not, I saw it. A single pinprick of warm, yellow light. Not a star. Stars are white and cold and they flicker. This was steady, and golden, and small, like a candle in an attic window seen from very far away.","I am not a sentimental man. I have sorted the undeliverable letters of this city for thirty-one years: letters to the drowned, to the wind, to people who moved away without a forwarding address. But I lifted my hand, there on the roof, and I waved. And the light, I swear it on my mother, the light went out and came back on again. Once. Twice. The way you flick a lamp to say goodnight to someone in the street.","The last letter came this morning. I have not yet decided whether to believe it, so I am printing it, and you may decide for me.",{letter:{date:"Tomorrow",text:"Thank you for waving. She cried when she saw it. She says to tell you the half-painted canvas in the attic is not finished because it was never meant to be: it is for you, the next one. And she says, and so do I: to whoever is reading this, on whatever night, in whatever window, please look up tonight. Not for long. Just long enough to wave. You cannot imagine how long I have been waiting for someone to wave back.",sign:"✦ M. & the Moon"}},"The letter was dated tomorrow. I have checked the calendar four times. Tonight, after I lock up, I am going to take that half-painted canvas home with me and set it up by my window. I do not know how to paint. I suspect that does not matter in the slightest.",{head:"The blank sheet"},"When I lifted the canvas from the easel, a single sheet of paper slid out from behind it and floated, quite slowly, to the floor. It was blank. Or it seemed blank, until the owl leaned down from the rafters and brushed it with the tip of one white wing, and for a moment, where the feather passed, I saw silver writing come and go. Moonlight ink. It only shows for those who wave something over it. I am printing it exactly as I found it. You will have to wave yourself.",{secret:{date:"The solstice, 1911",text:"To whoever finds the lamp: do not be afraid of the dark part of the moon. That is where the kindest light is kept. I have left you my brushes, my chair, and the half of her I never finished. Paint it however you feel; she always liked that best. And on clear nights, if you see a small gold light at her edge, do not wait for it to wave. I will be waving first.",sign:"Marguerite"}}]},{slug:"inside-the-brushmakers-workshop",title:"Inside the Brushmaker’s Workshop",kicker:"A Visit",deck:"Where wood remembers and brushes choose.",by:"Mara Kingsley",role:"Frame-mender of Lantern Row",minutes:9,img:"/images/scenes/gaz-workshop.webp",alt:"A candlelit workshop bench crowded with old tools and long wooden handles",caption:"The workbench, as I found it. I did not see the brushmaker light the candle. It was simply lit.",teaser:"Behind a nondescript door at the end of the lane lies a workshop where patience and intuition are carved into every handle. One frame-mender found the door that no map shows, and came home with a brush that remembers a name.",epigraph:"A brush is only wood and hair, until it remembers whose hand it was waiting for.",body:["There is a lane behind the fish market that does not appear on any map of the city, and I would not have found it at all if a wet black cat had not looked at me in a particular way and then walked into a wall. I followed it. The wall was not a wall. It was a narrow alley, so narrow that I had to turn sideways, and at the end of it there was a green door with no handle and a brass plate so worn that I could only read the last word: Brushes.","I knocked. The door opened before my knuckles had left it.","The brushmaker was smaller than I expected and older than seemed possible. His hands were as brown and knotted as willow roots, and his eyes were the clear, startling blue of a sky in a painting by someone who had never seen a real one. He did not ask who I was. He said, “You are late,” and stood aside.",{head:"Wood that remembers"},"Inside it smelled of linseed and beeswax and something older underneath, like a church after the candles have gone out. Brushes hung from every beam in bundles, thousands of them, and as I walked beneath them they stirred, all together, the way the grass stirs when a hawk passes over.","“They are curious,” the brushmaker said. “They do not get many visitors who are still alive.”","I laughed. He did not.","He showed me how it is done. The handles are cut only from willows that grow beside water, he said, because willow drinks, and a brush must be thirsty. The hairs are sable and badger and squirrel, gathered from creatures who gave them freely, combed from winter coats, picked from thorns. Each one is laid by hand, a single hair at a time, and as he worked he hummed a tune I almost knew, the kind of tune your mother hummed before you had words.","“And the brushes choose?” I asked, because that was what the old women of Lantern Row always said about his brushes, and I had always thought it was a figure of speech.","He put down his knife. “Come,” he said, “I will show you the drawer.”",{image:{src:"/images/scenes/gaz-books-ink.webp",alt:"An engraving of old books and an ink pot",side:"left"}},"At the back of the workshop was a cabinet of a hundred tiny drawers, each with a paper label written in a different hand. He opened one. Inside lay a single brush on a bed of faded velvet, and on the label: Agnes Hart, 1902, wanted to paint the sea.","“Every person who ever wanted to paint,” he said quietly, “and never did, leaves something behind when they die. A wish does not simply go out like a candle. It goes into the ground, and the willows drink it. I cut the willows. And the wish goes into the brush, and the brush remembers. It waits. Sometimes it waits a hundred years for the right hand: a child, a grandchild, a stranger with the same longing. And when that hand comes near, the brush knows.”",{quote:"A wish does not simply go out like a candle. It goes into the ground, and the willows drink it."},{head:"The drawer with my name"},"I wish I could tell you that I was sensible and kept a careful distance. I was not, and I did not. Because as he spoke, one of the little drawers near the bottom of the cabinet began to rattle. Gently at first, then insistently, like a bird in a box.","The brushmaker looked at me for a long time. Then he knelt, and pulled it open, and lifted out a small round brush with a handle the colour of honey, and held out the label so I could read it.","Eleanor Kingsley, 1971. Wanted to paint her garden.","Eleanor Kingsley was my grandmother. She was a seamstress. She sewed other people’s wedding dresses for forty-two years, and her fingers were stiff with it by the end, and I never once saw her draw so much as a flower. But she had a garden, a small one, behind the house, with hollyhocks taller than me, and in the summer evenings she would sit out there until it was too dark to see, just looking at it, with an expression I did not have a word for when I was a child. I have a word for it now. It is longing.","The brush was trembling in his palm. Not shaking: reaching. Towards me.","“Every brush has a price,” the brushmaker said. “Its first painting is not yours. It belongs to the one it remembers. After that, it is yours for life.” He folded my fingers around the handle. It was warm, as if someone had only just put it down.",{head:"The first painting"},"I mend frames; I do not fill them. I went home that night and bought a child’s box of watercolours from the corner shop, and I sat at my kitchen table and painted hollyhocks, badly, from memory. My hand did not feel like my hand. It moved more slowly and more surely than it ever has, with a patience I do not possess, and when I was finished it was almost midnight and the painting was, I can say without vanity because it was not mine, beautiful.","I left it to dry. In the morning there was someone in it.","Standing among the hollyhocks, in a cotton dress I recognised from a photograph on my mother’s mantelpiece, was a young woman of about twenty with my grandmother’s eyes. She was not looking at the garden. She was looking out of the painting, at me, and she was smiling, and one hand was lifted as if she had just that moment finished waving.","I went back to the lane behind the fish market to thank him. There was no lane. There was a wall, a real one this time, of old soot-black brick, and on it, at about the height of my heart, a small brass plaque, freshly polished: Brushmaker. Closed 1887.","The brush is beside me as I write this. It is my brush now; the price has been paid. And some nights, when I cannot sleep, I hear the faint sound of it rattling in its jar, very gently, like something that has remembered someone else."]},{slug:"whispers-after-midnight",title:"The Night the Portraits Stepped Out",kicker:"A Night Report",deck:"What the night reveals when the crowds are gone.",by:"Jasper Cale",role:"Apprentice Night-Watchman, the Galerie des Vernis",minutes:10,img:"/images/scenes/gaz-street.webp",alt:"A lamplit cobbled street at night with a lone cloaked figure",caption:"Rue des Vernis, a quarter to five in the morning. The figure in the distance is the author, still running.",teaser:"When the last shutters close and the last visitor departs, another side of the city emerges. A young night-watchman spends his first night on watch, the longest of the year, among the portraits, and learns the stories they only tell after midnight.",epigraph:"Some things can only be seen once. That is why they must be seen.",body:["The rule of the Galerie des Vernis is very simple, and the old night guard told it to me as he handed over his keys: you may stay all night, but you must not fall asleep, and whatever you see, you must not let the paintings out of your sight. I thought it was the sort of thing old night guards say to frighten new apprentices. He did not smile when he said it. He shook my hand for a long time, and then he went home, and I was alone with four hundred portraits and the sound of my own heart.","At midnight the clock in the east hall struck, and on the twelfth stroke somebody sneezed.","It was Madame Vernisse, the red-haired lady in the purple robe embroidered with stars, who hangs in the second room and who, it turns out, has opinions about everybody. “Dust,” she said, quite crossly, “nearly a hundred and forty years of it. Does nobody clean the frames any more?” And then, because I was staring at her with my mouth open: “Oh, close your mouth, young man, you look like a carp. Sir Viridian, wake up. We have a visitor, and he is wearing brown shoes with a black coat.”",{head:"The Turning"},"You may have heard that the portraits of the Galerie move when no one is looking. What you will not have heard, because the portraits made me promise not to tell it until now, is that one night every year, on the longest night, they may step out of their frames for a single hour. But only if a living person keeps watch, and does not sleep, and counts them all back in before the bell.","That night, I was the living person.","I do not have the words for that hour, though I have tried for weeks to find them. Dame Lazuli in her blue velvet came down out of her frame like someone stepping off a carriage, and Maestro Carmine bowed to her, and they danced in the empty hall to music only they could hear, her lace sweeping the floor, his old knees creaking. A Dutch merchant ate the painted grapes from a still life two rooms over and laughed until he cried. A general who had been painted on horseback for two centuries got down and simply lay on the cool marble floor with his arms spread out, saying, “Oh, my back. Oh, my back.”",{quote:"They danced in the empty hall to music only they could hear."},"And in the smallest room, in the smallest frame, there was a girl.","She was perhaps seven, painted in dark oils, in a nightgown, holding a candle, and she was the only one who did not come out. She just stood at the edge of her frame looking at the window, at the black square of night, as if she were waiting for something. The brass label beneath her said only: Wren. Painted by her father, 1689.","“Leave her be,” Dame Lazuli whispered to me as she waltzed past. “She has never seen anything but candles. Her father painted her after the fever took her, from memory, by candlelight. She has never seen a morning. None of us have, in here. The gallery has no windows facing east.”",{head:"The empty frame"},"When the clock struck one, the portraits went back. All of them, one after another, grumbling, yawning, the general complaining about his saddle, and I counted them in. Three hundred and ninety-nine. I counted again. Three hundred and ninety-nine.","The smallest frame was empty. There was only the dark painted wall, and a single drop of wax on the painted floor.","Sir Viridian, who is a critic and has never been kind about anything, went quite white beneath his varnish. “If a painting is not back in its frame by the first light of the sun,” he said, “it is neither painted nor living. It is simply gone. Every colour of her. Run, boy.”",{image:{src:"/images/scenes/gaz-castle-ink.webp",alt:"An engraving of towers and rooftops at night",side:"right"}},"I ran. The back door was open, and there were small wet footprints on the cobbles, made of oil paint, a dark umber brown. I followed them through the sleeping city, down the Rue des Vernis, past the shuttered caf\xe9s and the cathedral and the bridge, and they did not go anywhere you would expect a lost child to go. They went east. Always east. Uphill.","I found her at a quarter to five, on the top of the old city wall, sitting with her painted knees drawn up under her nightgown and her candle beside her, looking at the place where the sky was beginning, very faintly, to turn grey.","“You have to come back,” I said. I was out of breath. “Wren, please. The sun. You will disappear.”","She looked up at me with eyes that were only brushstrokes and were somehow, impossibly, the most alive eyes I have ever seen. “I know,” she said. “Father told me about mornings. He said they were the most beautiful thing in the world. He said I would see a hundred thousand of them. I have only ever seen candles.” She turned back to the east. “Just one,” she said. “Please. I would like to see just one.”",{head:"One sunrise"},"I was the watchman that night, and I am supposed to tell you what a watchman should have done. I should have picked her up and carried her back. I think I could have made it. Instead I sat down beside her on the cold stones, and I took off my coat and put it around her shoulders, and we watched.","It came up slowly, the way it always does, the way you and I have seen it come up so many times that we no longer bother to look. First the grey, then a thin line of rose along the rooftops, then gold, then a great spill of light across the river and the bridges and the wet slate roofs, until the whole city looked as if it had been painted that minute by someone who loved it very much.","Wren did not say anything. She only held my sleeve and watched, and as the light reached us she began to grow pale, then golden, then pale again, the colours lifting off her like mist off a pond. She was smiling. At the very end, when there was hardly anything left of her but the outline of a smile and the warmth of a small hand on my sleeve, she whispered: “Oh. Father was right.”","And then my coat was empty.","I walked back to the gallery in the full morning light, and I could not see the street for crying. I unlocked the smallest room to face the empty frame and tell the others I had failed them.","The frame was not empty.","Wren was in it. But she was no longer a child in the dark with a candle. She stood on a city wall in a coat several sizes too big for her, in a blaze of sunrise painted in colours I have never seen on any canvas: rose and saffron and a gold so bright it hurt to look at. The candle was gone. She did not need it any more. And when I put my hand very close to the canvas, not touching, I promise I did not touch, it was warm.","The new catalogue lists the painting as Wren at Dawn, artist unknown. The guards say it has always hung there. If you visit, you must not touch it. But if you stand very still in the smallest room, late in the afternoon, you will notice that the painted sun in it is slowly setting, and that every morning, without anyone seeing, it rises again."]}],n={kicker:"From The Living Star Atlas",title:"Tales of the Stars",intro:"The same stars, seen by many peoples. Persian poets and Greek shepherds, Māori navigators and Kiowa grandmothers each looked up at the same lights and told a different story. Here they all are, retold in full.",jump:e=>"And ".concat(e," tales of the stars, told by many peoples ↓"),people:"Whose sky?",sky:"Which stars?",all:"All",allSky:"The whole sky",shown:e=>1===e?"One tale":"".concat(e," tales"),read:"Read the tale →",minutes:e=>"".concat(e," min read"),none:"No tale yet for these stars from this people. The atlas grows, night by night.",atlas:"See these stars tonight, in The Living Star Atlas →"}}}]);